00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:50.860
Prison food is institutional food

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.860
That other institutional food laughs at

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.660
No, that's actually not true, it sneers at it

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:57.660
And with good reason

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:02.660
The prison authorities don't want to spend a single dollar more than they have to

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:05.060
Fold in a bunch of competing interests

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.060
Plenty of corruption and greed

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:11.860
And the result is a hellish jambalaya of gastronomic awfulness

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:16.860
There was one incident where for taco night, they accidentally fed us dog food

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:23.860
The reason we know that is that the warden, months after the fact, sent everybody a memo saying

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:30.860
Hey, sorry, but a couple of months ago on taco night, we accidentally fed you dog food

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:35.860
It's not our fault, the packaging was mixed up at the meat plant

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:36.860
Yeah

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:40.860
They labeled dog food as ground beef and they labeled the ground beef as dog food

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:45.860
The shame is not even that we were fed dog food

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:50.860
The shame is that we were fed dog food and we couldn't even tell the difference

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:52.860
So what did we do?

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:56.860
We relied on people like our guest tonight, Joey Castlell

00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:03.860
Joey Castlell was one of the finest, I'm going to call him chefs at FCI Loretto

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.860
A lot of guys cook, the Mexicans all cook, right?

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:14.860
You have a trash can of water, you throw in a live electrical wire called a stinger

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:18.860
It immediately brings the water to a boil, you put some food in a plastic bag

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:21.860
Next thing you know, you've got a gourmet meal

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:23.860
The Mexicans ate to survive

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:28.860
We ate, and I say we, I'm including myself with the Italians

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.860
We ate as a culinary experience

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:31.860
Yes

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.860
Joey, welcome, good to have you

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:35.860
Thank you, John

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:37.860
Joey, walk us through this man

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:42.860
When I first saw a stinger, I unplugged it

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:44.860
I said, oh my God, what a health hazard

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:48.860
This is a live electrical wire, and the Mexicans are like, are you out of your mind?

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:50.860
We're making dinner

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:52.860
I didn't know what the heck they were doing

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:55.860
But then when you cooked, when Mark Lanzalotti cooked

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:58.860
It was like being in a restaurant in Little Italy

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.860
How did you do that? Walk us through it

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:05.860
Well, when I first came to the prison, let me say this real quick

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:08.860
I didn't know nothing about prison just like you

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:09.860
Sure

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:12.860
So I walked in, and this guy comes up to me

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:17.860
I forget his name, a black guy, and he says, what's your name, sir?

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:22.860
I said, Joey, and I'm thinking to myself, wow, nice people here

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.860
They're talking to me

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:27.860
So everybody's laughing, and I'm like, what are you laughing at?

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:30.860
He says, we'll tell you later, just keep talking to this guy

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:35.860
So I'm talking to him, he says, if there's anything you need, Joey, you let me know

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.860
And I said, okay

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:42.860
So then I go back to myself, three guys come in that were laughing

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:45.860
And I said, can you tell me why were you laughing?

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:48.860
He says, the guy is gay

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:53.860
He wants to pick you up right away because you're new in the prison

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:57.860
Well, the word got out to Peter Calabrese

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:01.860
When he found out, he said, Joey, stay away from them guys

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:06.860
If you find a Twinkie or a piece of gum or a lollipop on your bed, don't touch it

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:08.860
I said, okay, Pete, I won't

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:11.860
So I was learning little things here and there

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:16.860
And with the cooking, I knew how to cook on the outside with the stove

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:19.860
But like John said, the stinger

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:22.860
I knew what that was, so I bought one one day

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:25.860
Oh, you're kidding me, so you didn't know what a stinger was?

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:29.860
Watching, watching, everybody cook, I said, oh, okay

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:33.860
But the stuff they cooked was rice and the same things

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:34.860
Yeah, yeah

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:38.860
I cooked pizzas, I cooked managotti, I cooked spaghetti, meatballs

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:44.860
John, it was two o'clock in the morning, John sound asleep

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:46.860
I said, I'm hungry, I'm gonna cook

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:51.860
I made, I think I made a pizza or managotti, something I made Italian

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:54.860
So I crawled out, I walked to John's room

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:58.860
I said, John, John, he goes, yeah, I'm sleeping

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:01.860
I said, John, I got Italian food

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:03.860
He says Italian food and he got right up

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:05.860
I did, I did

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:09.860
And this went on consistently, we did it all the time

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:12.860
Yeah, how about the calzones you used to make?

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:15.860
Let me talk about the calzones for a second

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:19.860
So a lot of the Mexican guys used to cook

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:22.860
And like you said, it was rice, rice, rice, rice

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:26.860
With this cezon sauce, or not even sauce, it was like an orange powder

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:30.860
That had no flavor at all, but they sold it in the commissary

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:34.860
And so they would sell these, they would call them calzones

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:38.860
And Pete said to me one day, why do you buy those disgusting things?

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:42.860
And I said, oh, they're so filling and delicious

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:44.860
They got all kinds of stuff in them

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:47.860
He goes, it's not a calzone, it's a fucking burrito

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:51.860
Those guys wouldn't know a calzone if it walked up and introduced it itself to them

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:52.860
Yes

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:53.860
And so I said, you're right

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:56.860
And then you started making calzones and I said, oh my god

00:09:56.860 --> 00:10:00.860
This is an actual calzone, like from an Italian restaurant

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:04.860
Made in a trash bucket, full of boiling water

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:07.860
But wait a minute, I don't know if you knew this

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:10.860
I never got, I got some of the stuff from commissary

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:13.860
I used to get it from the guards in the kitchen

